    # AUREATE GUILD-CERTIFIED FIRE-CORE BRAZIER (MODEL: ZENITH-III)
    **Vendor:** Aureate (merchant Guilds-born), Dawn Naong Distribution. Stillness-Bound warranty protocols. Batch 7-Calving Restock. Includes one (1) fire-core, rated 90-day burn. 14 aurora-mark.
    
    ---
    
    **★★★★★ — Yijuhua (dawn-found), Upper-Third Alignment, Terrace-level crèche manager**
    
    Purchased two Zenith-IIIs for the eastern nursery annexe after the old spire-wall heating failed — you know why it failed, we all know why. I will say this plainly: fourteen marks is a cruelty. But the units arrived sealed with proper crystalline certification, the cores were warm to the flipper on delivery, and my chicks stopped coughing within a night-cycle. Burn has been consistent for forty days now. Aureate used to be the only name worth trusting and I suppose they still are, if you can stomach what trust costs since everything shifted. The ice remembers what we paid. The silver-threaded coats were there when the Spiral Council was still counting bodies — I will not forget that. But fourteen marks. Would recommend if you have the marks. If you don't — well. I don't know what to tell you.
    
    ---
    
    **★☆☆☆☆ — Blizzard Jangshingfa (frost-born) xi'Dawn, Mid-Fifth Alignment, salvage engineer**
    
    Documentation for collective review: as described, but — the core was cold-burned on arrival. Discoloration running deep, like something from the Still Pools got into it. I've handled fire-cores for twelve cycles. I know what corruption threading looks like, and this core had it: faint whisper-harmonic when I held it near the nursery wall, wrong colour in the glow. Pre-collapse fire-core pricing through Aureate held at eleven crystal-weights per unit. Current rate: fourteen. I document the increase not to accuse but because those of us remaining must track what we pay for survival. Reported the corruption to the Dawn Naong office. They sent a form. A FORM. Aureate certified this batch themselves — their seal, their abacus-mark, their Stillness-Bound guarantee. The guarantee says replacement within three days. It has been nineteen. My settlement is heating on salvage scraps. Let the ledgers speak. The Luminaries may draw conclusions. The Aureate my mother bought from doesn't exist anymore. What's left just wears its coat.
    
    ---
    
    **★★★☆☆ — Cheixizhi (aurora-blessed) zhulong'Crystal, Lower-Fourth Alignment, workshop artificer**
    
    Fair review. The brazier housing is solid work — better than anything Holdborn (ice-thresh) is dumping at the Drowned Ledge for nine marks, though I'll admit I was tempted. Those Codex-stamped cores at fire-sale prices — you know what I mean. You get what you pay for with disputed provenance, and I can't afford to find out the hard way. The Aureate core itself burned clean for the first thirty days, then started dimming faster than the rated curve. I weighed it at purchase: proper mass, proper certificate. But Nightshelf sourcing has been unreliable since the mine collapses, and I suspect Aureate is padding inventory with shorter-burn stock and hoping we won't measure. Some of us measure. For standard transactions, their Stillness-Bound protocols prevent the disputes that plagued my grandfather's generation. For a workshop that doesn't run overnight, adequate. For a crèche or a home with chicks? Spend the extra marks for a batch-verified unit, or find someone who reads crystal quality better than Aureate's auditors do. I know a grey-coat trader who showed me an uncut sample that put the Zenith-III to shame, but I won't say more than that. The monopoly functions. Whether it serves is a separate question. Qualified recommendation.
    
    ---
    
    **QUICK TAKES:**
    
    **★★★★☆** — *Angnongei (light-descended) Vorwi'*
    Good enough. Still trading when others weren't. My flipper-brother needed fire-cores for the hatchlings and got them same-cycle. Got mine from a source. Not Aureate. Not Holdborn (ice-thresh) Core still burns warm after sixty days. Don't ask about the flipper-shaking. Prices hurt but prices always hurt now.
    
    **★☆☆☆☆** — *name withheld*
    needed this after evacuation. paid what they asked. core died in 22 days not 90. chick is sick again. the accord is supposed to protect us.
    
    **★★★★★** — *Xehinghawei (dawn-found) Skael', formerly of Holdborn (ice-thresh) holdings*
    Left Holdborn (ice-thresh) when they couldn't maintain resource security during membership. Aureate at least delivers what they promise. The codex never kept anyone warm. Discretion appreciated. Availability consistent. Transaction speed acceptable despite representative's visible tremor. Do not mention former associates if prompt service matters.
    
    **★★★☆☆** — *Ledger-Warden, retired*
    Acceptable unit. Ingnao would have rejected this batch. But Ingnao is filed away where none of us can reach, and the ledgers never balance.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771581980650_0", anchor: "Nightshelf sourcing has been unreliable since the mine collapses", text: "The entire survival architecture of two civilizations — aurora-worshippers on the surface, fire-miners in the volcanic depths, bound by the Flipper Accord's mutual dependency — compressed into a workshop artificer's supply complaint. Cheixizhi is not wrong. She is describing an existential crisis in the tone one uses for late deliveries. I find this the most honest register available.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771581980650_1", anchor: "representative's visible tremor", text: "Qeilin-Qingwei was Holdborn's crystal-reader until the schism. Her flippers shake — this is documented in contexts I have annotated elsewhere. Xehinghawei, *formerly of Holdborn*, buys from a trembling Aureate representative and advises against mentioning former associates. Meanwhile Cheixizhi won't name the grey-coat trader whose uncut sample outperformed Aureate's certified stock. A crystal-reader would know quality by touch. If Qeilin-Qingwei is selling through Aureate's front while undercutting it through grey-coat channels, she has made herself the indispensable middleman of a collapsing supply chain. The schism didn't ruin her. It freed her.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771581980650_2", anchor: "the accord is supposed to protect us", text: "It is supposed to. The second secret draft contained provisions for exactly this — emergency redistribution during supply failures. That draft was never ratified. What was ratified protects trade routes, not buyers.", type: :correction, display: :popout
